WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.381
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.660
DIANE GREENE: Hey, Paul.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:10.310
PAUL DAUGHTERY: Hey, Diane.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:10.790
DIANE GREENE: Nice to see you.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:11.900
PAUL DAUGHTERY:
Great to see you.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:12.691
DIANE GREENE: Yeah.

00:00:12.691 --> 00:00:14.607
I first heard of
Paul because he's

00:00:14.607 --> 00:00:16.690
a board of trustee at the
Computer History Museum.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.250
And then whoa, he's
the CTO of Accenture.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.320
And I've been
getting to know Paul.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.860
And I found out he started
out as a programmer-- software

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.730
engineer-- went on to
become a computer science,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:33.650
entered the PhD program-- took
a little time out at Accenture.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:36.680
And bang, 30 years later,
you're still there.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.700
I think you and [INAUDIBLE]
may be the only two

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.310
people in this facility that
have never worked anywhere else

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:47.920
but where you're currently
working, outside of academia.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.770
Tell me, how did that happen?

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.309
PAUL DAUGHTERY:
Well, I got into it.

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:53.100
First, I was at the
University of Michigan.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.200
And one day-- I vividly
remember the day

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.800
when [INAUDIBLE] computer
showed up on campus.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.160
And I saw those and said,
this is what I'm going to do.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.390
And I got hooked on software
and computers at that point.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.239
And I was going to go
into a PhD program,

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:06.420
as I talked to you about.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.920
And I had a mentor
that said, go get

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.480
one of your business experience
before you get your PhD.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.680
And I very strategically went
to the placement office and went

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.320
down the list, and
started in the A's.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.110
And I work for Accenture.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.690
And I thought it'd be one
year of coding COBOL and being

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.180
miserable with that, and then
going back to the PhD program.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.080
I showed up on my first project,
which was a driver licensing

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.370
system for a state
government in the US,

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:34.190
and COBOL, IMS, DB/DC, and
my worst nightmares realized.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.785
And I had a smart
group of folks I

00:01:36.785 --> 00:01:39.460
was working with that came up
with an approach to say, let's

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.450
not code this thing.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.680
Let's model it in
Smalltalk as objects.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.460
And let's generate the
schemas for the database

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.665
and generate the COBOL code.

00:01:46.665 --> 00:01:48.324
And it'll be
verifiable, and it'll

00:01:48.324 --> 00:01:49.490
be a better way of doing it.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:50.156
And we did that.

00:01:50.156 --> 00:01:51.850
And it just turned
me on to this idea

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.225
that we can use
very innovative--

00:01:53.225 --> 00:01:56.160
and that was 1986-- use
very innovative technologies

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.970
like Smalltalk,
even at that stage,

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.060
to solve real-world problems
and bring innovation

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.610
to results close together--
and got hooked on it.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.630
And I'm still here now.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.200
DIANE GREENE: Lucky Accenture.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:12.220
So 30 years-- you've seen a lot
of transitions in technology.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:14.740
So where would you
put cloud in that?

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.620
What was the tipping point?

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.470
And also, when did
you at Accenture

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.160
decide Accenture should
embrace the cloud

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.785
and start building solutions?

00:02:25.785 --> 00:02:27.410
PAUL DAUGHTERY: For
me, it's economics.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:28.960
If you look at economics,
you can figure out

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:29.620
what's going to happen.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.090
And you and
[INAUDIBLE] and others

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:32.715
talked about this
already this morning.

00:02:32.715 --> 00:02:36.710
But we had a fundamental belief
going back almost 20 years ago

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.919
that compute,
storage, and network

00:02:38.919 --> 00:02:41.460
costs were going to go to zero,
asymptotically approach zero.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.170
And that's what's happening.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.790
And we believe that
as that happens,

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.690
you'd have things like
the cloud happening,

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.410
even though we didn't call
it cloud at that point,

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.118
and would have things
like IoT happening,

00:02:50.118 --> 00:02:51.790
because you either
centralize for power

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.700
or you distribute with
small devices at the edge.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.417
And so we have been
investing and driving

00:02:57.417 --> 00:02:59.250
in that direction for
a long period of time.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:02.020
And then about 2008, we
looked at it and said,

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:04.370
there's a big commercial
business around this

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:05.490
that we can form.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.850
And you can see the
slide up there, I think,

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.690
that talks about the
evolution of technology

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.405
that you and I
talked about, Diane.

00:03:11.405 --> 00:03:14.390
And what we saw was the
ability to really form

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:15.710
a big business around cloud.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:20.022
So we launched a strategic
initiative around 2008--

00:03:20.022 --> 00:03:21.480
said, let's get
serious about this.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.710
And that's grown into being
one of the biggest and most

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.510
successful parts of Accenture,
which is our cloud business--

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.950
so over 30,000 people,
20,000 projects completed

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:31.670
in driving cloud.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.220
And then about two years ago,
we saw this pivot, I think,

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.900
that many in the audience
have probably seen,

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.149
and that some of the speakers
have talked about today,

00:03:38.149 --> 00:03:39.870
where we believed
about two years ago,

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.350
we saw that public cloud
first was the way to go.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:44.045
We started shifting our focus.

00:03:44.045 --> 00:03:45.920
We had been doing a lot
of public cloud work.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.160
We said, that's
the way to start,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.070
and let's just use that
as a starting point.

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:53.195
And we've been driving on that
mission for about two years.

00:03:53.195 --> 00:03:54.820
And that gets us to
where we are today.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.195
But the important
thing, I think,

00:03:56.195 --> 00:03:57.970
and where it comes
to Google is, is

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.755
the cloud is really a
starting point, in our view.

00:04:00.755 --> 00:04:03.380
And you can see all the waves of
innovation that are happening.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:06.120
And what that means for
people running businesses

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.369
is you need a platform where
you can innovate faster.

00:04:08.369 --> 00:04:10.160
And all the innovations--
machine learning,

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.247
artificial intelligence,
and innovations to come--

00:04:13.247 --> 00:04:15.080
are why you need the
cloud as the foundation

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.810
because no business can innovate
fast enough on their own

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.649
without tapping into
the power of a cloud,

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:21.060
like what we've put together.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.550
DIANE GREENE: Getting the data,
getting the mobile access,

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.040
bringing it all together.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:26.831
PAUL DAUGHTERY: Right.

00:04:26.831 --> 00:04:28.080
DIANE GREENE: It's a big deal.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.774
PAUL DAUGHTERY: Yeah,
it's a big deal.

00:04:29.774 --> 00:04:31.440
It's a big challenge
for the enterprises

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.635
to do it fast enough, given the
pace of technology [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:33.635 --> 00:04:34.468
DIANE GREENE: Right.

00:04:34.468 --> 00:04:36.260
Right.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:40.030
And the other thing I
was talking about today

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:44.770
is getting into this exponential
curve of having impact,

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.550
and how we feel working
with our partners,

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.720
working with our customers,
we're now able to do that.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.160
And we've been in the
cloud for over 16 years.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.340
But I mentioned as
you were coming out

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.970
that we're now announcing
our partnership where

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.380
we're going to bring
solutions to our customers.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:04.290
Can you tell me a
little bit about that?

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:04.480
PAUL DAUGHTERY: Yeah.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.660
No, we're very excited
about the partnership

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:08.790
that you and I have
been working on.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:12.310
And for me, our business is
about bringing innovation

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.190
to customers to help them
transform their businesses

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:16.490
for the digital economy.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.740
And when we look across
everything you've talked about

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.650
and laid out-- and my
compliments on the way

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.360
you've laid out the story--
it's really fantastic with--

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:22.790
DIANE GREENE: Oh, good.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.915
PAUL DAUGHTERY: --Google
cloud and the positioning.

00:05:24.915 --> 00:05:26.330
DIANE GREENE: It's true-- real.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:26.800
PAUL DAUGHTERY: It's real.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.600
And we see a
tremendous opportunity

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.200
to bring all that technology
together collectively to really

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.260
help transform business.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:34.718
And that's
fundamentally what we're

00:05:34.718 --> 00:05:37.110
doing through the
partnership we announced.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.280
So when you look
at Google Cloud,

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:41.740
you look at apps, the
analytics, look at ML,

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:45.240
at Android, Chrome, Maps,
all the pieces together--

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.050
Tango-- you look at the
transformative power

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:49.920
of the business,
that's what excites us.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.370
And so we formed
the partnership.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:55.170
And we're launching an Accenture
and Google Joint Innovation

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.400
Center to bring things
to life for customers.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.110
We're forming an
Accenture-Google Joint Center

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.204
of Excellence to
build those solutions

00:06:02.204 --> 00:06:03.120
and develop solutions.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.910
And we're focusing on a set
of solutions and products

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:10.305
that will help clients construct
better digital experiences,

00:06:10.305 --> 00:06:11.930
will help transform
the digital culture

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:13.464
within their
organizations, and then

00:06:13.464 --> 00:06:14.880
bring better digital
tools to help

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.130
them manage in their business.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:17.450
And it's very exciting.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:19.730
DIANE GREENE: Any
specific examples?

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.460
I think it's so cool
that you're doing

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:24.640
both Android and the
cloud, and the apps

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.475
in this sort of broad
array-- the machine learning.

00:06:27.475 --> 00:06:28.350
PAUL DAUGHTERY: Yeah.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.000
There's a number
of great examples

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.870
that I get really excited about.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.869
One of the ones I get
particularly excited about

00:06:32.869 --> 00:06:35.150
is something we're calling
Retail Trend Watcher.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.300
And Retail Trend Watcher is--
it combines some unique IP

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.800
and research we've done around
something called the customer

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:44.400
genome, which is a unique way
of understanding the customer.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.370
And we're putting
that on Google Cloud.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:50.030
And then we're combining a
lot of other capabilities.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:52.900
So we're tying in the Vision
API so that we can do things

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:55.790
like go to Pinterest and
find images and pictures

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:59.520
and categorize-- get the
item/product classifications

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:00.020
and things.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:01.811
And we're adding the
natural language APIs.

00:07:01.811 --> 00:07:04.680
So we go to Facebook and find
customer comments and sentiment

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:05.870
and those sorts of things.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:08.750
And you put that all together
with the ML-- the Machine

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:12.190
Learning, the API-- to do
the new neural modeling

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:14.430
to then do forecasting and
trend prediction of what's

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:15.680
going to happen going forward.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.680
We think this is just a
transformative tool set,

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.270
and a great solution
for retail customers.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:21.811
DIANE GREENE: We're
going to give you

00:07:21.811 --> 00:07:23.454
an honorary PhD for doing that.

00:07:23.454 --> 00:07:24.372
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:24.372 --> 00:07:26.080
PAUL DAUGHTERY: I
could use one of those.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:27.612
Thanks.

00:07:27.612 --> 00:07:28.320
DIANE GREENE: No.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.820
So another interesting thing
I learned about you is you've

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:36.450
been really paying attention
to the trends in the workplace.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:41.750
We saw with Airbus, here they
were analyzing these satellite

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.860
images with people by hand.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.040
It took a long time.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:46.190
It took a lot of people.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:48.050
And then they applied
machine learning.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:51.200
And it really didn't take
so many people, or very long

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:52.310
anymore.

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:54.730
Some people are kind of
saying the workplace is

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:56.160
getting disrupted.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.330
I know you talked about that
at the World Economic Forum.

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:04.290
And you really take a
very optimistic view.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.180
I actually share it.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.550
I wonder if you could talk
a little bit about that.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:08.660
PAUL DAUGHTERY: Yeah.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.010
I'll just give you the
summary, and starting it

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:14.660
by saying that I agree with
some others who have said today

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.940
that artificial intelligence
is the big thing that's

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.990
going to transform business
over the next 20 years-- bigger

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:22.000
than anything else we've seen.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.470
And the way we've done business
in the 20th century, which

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:26.120
is the way most of us
are still doing business,

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:28.330
just isn't going to work
anymore going forward.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.540
So we have this
tremendous transformation

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:31.956
of business powered by cloud
and powered by the innovation

00:08:31.956 --> 00:08:33.190
that we're talking about.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:35.745
And that's certainly going to
create a lot of disruption.

00:08:35.745 --> 00:08:37.370
But it's really going
to create growth.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.650
And we just released
a study yesterday

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:41.789
that's out there,
available on our website,

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:45.252
where we did a modeling of what
the economic impact of AI is,

00:08:45.252 --> 00:08:46.270
and the business impact.

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:48.470
And what we showed
with this study is

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:52.270
that we believe that there'll
be a potential doubling of GDP,

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:55.030
which in the US would mean a
growth from 2 and 1/2% to 4

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:57.960
and 1/2%, roughly, which
could be $8 trillion

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.956
of added economic output in the
economy, if the potential of AI

00:09:01.956 --> 00:09:04.080
is realized-- similar
results in Japan and Germany,

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.840
and most other
countries we looked at.

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:07.280
And the underlying
reason for that

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.760
is that we believe AI drives
increased productivity

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:12.819
of up to 40% for individuals
and people and humans,

00:09:12.819 --> 00:09:15.110
depending on the jobs you're
doing, because it's people

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:17.040
plus the technology
coming together,

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.800
allowing people to do what
they do best more effectively.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:20.770
So we believe it's
a tremendous story.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:21.950
It creates economic growth.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:23.324
It creates growth
for businesses,

00:09:23.324 --> 00:09:25.380
growth for economies,
and better opportunities

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:26.820
for people going forward.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.403
DIANE GREENE: And
it's good that we're

00:09:28.403 --> 00:09:30.610
teaching every
kindergartner how to program

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:33.110
so that as they get up
there, all people will

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:34.524
be participating in this.

00:09:34.524 --> 00:09:36.690
PAUL DAUGHTERY: Will be
better-- that's a big thing.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.380
If you don't do the right things
from an education perspective--

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:39.932
and it needs to
start early-- then

00:09:39.932 --> 00:09:41.682
we won't have the
workforce prepared well.

00:09:41.682 --> 00:09:42.710
So that really is critical.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:43.980
We're involved with
a lot of initiatives

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:44.970
to make sure that's the case.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:47.303
DIANE GREENE: But we definitely
don't have enough people

00:09:47.303 --> 00:09:49.260
to-- very opportunity-rich
world we're

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:50.745
living in-- very exciting.

00:09:50.745 --> 00:09:51.470
PAUL DAUGHTERY: Yep, many times.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:52.790
DIANE GREENE: Well,
thanks a lot, Paul.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:53.510
This is great.

